    <div class="max-w-3xl mb-20">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold mb-6">The problem nobody explains to landscapers</h2>
      <p class="text-gray-500 leading-relaxed mb-5">You do not have a storefront. Your customers never come to you — you go to them. In Google's world that makes you a <strong class="text-gray-800">service-area business</strong>, and service-area businesses rank differently from a restaurant or a dentist.</p>
      <p class="text-gray-500 leading-relaxed mb-5">Here is the part that frustrates people: Google still ranks you in the map using the distance between the searcher and <em>your verified address</em>, even when that address is hidden and even when you have listed a fifty-mile service area. Setting a wide service area tells Google where you are willing to work. It does not make you show up there.</p>
      <p class="text-gray-500 leading-relaxed mb-5">This is why a landscaping company in one town can dominate its own zip code and be invisible fifteen minutes away, no matter how many service areas it lists. It is not a bug and there is no trick that fixes it.</p>
      <p class="text-gray-500 leading-relaxed">What you can do is win decisively where you actually are, and use website pages — not the map — to reach the neighborhoods further out. Two different tools for two different distances. An agency that does not explain this distinction is going to sell you a fifty-mile service area and a monthly invoice.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="grid lg:grid-cols-2 gap-16 mb-20">
      <div>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold mb-6">The three things Google is weighing</h2>
        <p class="text-gray-500 leading-relaxed mb-5"><strong class="text-gray-800">Distance</strong> is the one you cannot change without moving. It is also the reason chasing rankings in a town an hour away is usually wasted money.</p>
        <p class="text-gray-500 leading-relaxed mb-5"><strong class="text-gray-800">Relevance</strong> is how well your profile matches what was typed. This is the one most landscaping companies get wrong, and the fix is often a five-minute change.</p>
        <p class="text-gray-500 leading-relaxed"><strong class="text-gray-800">Prominence</strong> is how established you look — reviews, mentions of your business elsewhere on the web, an active profile. This is the slow one, and the one that compounds.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-3xl p-8" style="background:var(--primary-lt)">
        <p class="text-xs font-semibold tracking-widest uppercase text-gray-400 mb-5">The category mistake</p>
        <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">Google keeps separate categories for <strong class="text-gray-800">Landscaper</strong>, <strong class="text-gray-800">Lawn care service</strong>, <strong class="text-gray-800">Landscape designer</strong>, <strong class="text-gray-800">Tree service</strong> and <strong class="text-gray-800">Lawn irrigation equipment supplier</strong>. They are not interchangeable.</p>
        <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">Your primary category carries far more weight than the secondary ones. A company set to "Landscaper" that makes most of its money on weekly mowing is competing in the wrong box — and a company set to "Lawn care service" that wants hardscape work is doing the reverse.</p>
        <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">Pick the primary for the work you want more of, not the work you do most of. Then add the rest as secondary.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="mb-20">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold mb-3">What we do, in order</h2>
      <p class="text-gray-500 leading-relaxed mb-8 max-w-3xl">Roughly the first ninety days. The order matters more than people think — reviews sent to an unverified profile are wasted, and content built before the categories are right points at the wrong audience.</p>
      <div class="grid sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-3 gap-5">
        <div class="service-card reveal rounded-2xl border border-gray-100 p-6 bg-white shadow-sm">
          <h3 class="font-semibold mb-2 text-gray-900">1 · Profile and categories</h3>
          <p class="text-sm text-gray-400 leading-relaxed">Verification, primary category chosen for the work you want, secondaries filled in, service list matched to what you actually sell and price.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="service-card reveal rounded-2xl border border-gray-100 p-6 bg-white shadow-sm" style="transition-delay:.05s">
          <h3 class="font-semibold mb-2 text-gray-900">2 · Name, address, phone</h3>
          <p class="text-sm text-gray-400 leading-relaxed">Identical across every directory that feeds Google. Not similar — identical, down to whether you write Street or St. Inconsistency here quietly caps everything above it.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="service-card reveal rounded-2xl border border-gray-100 p-6 bg-white shadow-sm" style="transition-delay:.10s">
          <h3 class="font-semibold mb-2 text-gray-900">3 · Real job photos</h3>
          <p class="text-sm text-gray-400 leading-relaxed">Shot on site, uploaded steadily rather than fifty at once. Before-and-afters of install work do more for the profile than any stock image ever will.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="service-card reveal rounded-2xl border border-gray-100 p-6 bg-white shadow-sm" style="transition-delay:.15s">
          <h3 class="font-semibold mb-2 text-gray-900">4 · A review habit</h3>
          <p class="text-sm text-gray-400 leading-relaxed">Built into how a job closes, so they arrive weekly. Recency matters more than the lifetime total, and a steady trickle beats a burst every time.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="service-card reveal rounded-2xl border border-gray-100 p-6 bg-white shadow-sm" style="transition-delay:.20s">
          <h3 class="font-semibold mb-2 text-gray-900">5 · Neighborhood pages</h3>
          <p class="text-sm text-gray-400 leading-relaxed">For the areas the map will not reach. One page per place worth owning, each genuinely about that place — not the same paragraph with the town name swapped.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="service-card reveal rounded-2xl border border-gray-100 p-6 bg-white shadow-sm" style="transition-delay:.25s">
          <h3 class="font-semibold mb-2 text-gray-900">6 · A report you can read</h3>
          <p class="text-sm text-gray-400 leading-relaxed">Where you rank in each area, which way it moved, how many calls came from the profile. One page, plain language, monthly.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="max-w-3xl mb-20">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold mb-6">The same-name-swapped trap</h2>
      <p class="text-gray-500 leading-relaxed mb-5">Nearly every landscaping website we audit has the same flaw. Somebody built four or five town pages by copying one and changing the name. They read fine to a person. To Google they are near-duplicates, so it cannot tell which one to show — and instead of ranking one page well, it ranks several of them badly.</p>
      <p class="text-gray-500 leading-relaxed">We have measured this on real sites: five pages from the same company competing for one search, the best sitting at position 27 and the rest trailing behind it into the seventies. Consolidating that mess and writing genuinely different pages is often the single biggest available gain, and it costs nothing but the writing.</p>
    </div>

